{"turn":1,"elapsedMs":65034,"answer":"**IL6 is the gene; IL-6 is the cytokine protein it encodes.** Gene expression is assessed through IL6 RNA in specified cells or tissue. A blood IL-6 assay measures circulating protein concentration, not RNA. DNA methylation, genotype and receptor signalling are separate measurements. [S1]\n\n**No—a blood IL-6 change does not prove changed gene expression in the brain.** Protein concentration reflects production, release and clearance. Brain-expression claims need brain-specific RNA evidence; even blood-cell RNA does not directly establish brain expression. [S1]\n\n**Trust Factor crosses summarise study-design coverage:**\n- **One cross:** peer-reviewed research.\n- **Two crosses:** randomised controlled trials, including single-masked trials.\n- **Three crosses:** verified double-masked randomised controlled trials.\n\nChapter/subchapter ratings use the highest level supported by at least **three distinct qualifying studies**. Linked reports from one trial count once. Chapter 14 has three crosses; individual subchapters differ. Crosses are **not grades of certainty, causality or clinical benefit**. [S1] For example, the double-blind schizophrenia tocilizumab trial found no symptom or cognitive improvement. [S2]","chargedUpperUSD":0.8538375}
